WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960
What if Claude Code had exactly the

2
00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.360
button, panel, or shortcut you’ve always wanted?

3
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.060
Now it

4
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:06.820
can.

5
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.740
Claude Code Mods launched on October 1.

6
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.000
The idea is simple:

7
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.660
you describe a feature you’d like,

8
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.620
and Claude Code builds it right inside

9
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:15.360
itself.

10
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.240
I’m Nei Maldaner,

11
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.260
and in this video I’ll explain what

12
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:18.940
a mod is,

13
00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.220
how it works under the hood,

14
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.520
what you can do with it,

15
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.060
and how to create your own,

16
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.340
along with a few precautions worth taking.

17
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.480
A mod is a small add-on that

18
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:29.600
changes two things:

19
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.420
how Claude Code appears on your screen

20
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.820
and how it works.

21
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.860
It runs in the terminal and in

22
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.080
the Code tab of the desktop app.

23
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.800
One detail to keep in mind:

24
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.100
it doesn’t work in regular Claude chat.

25
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.720
Mods are for Claude Code.

26
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.120
If you’ve already updated,

27
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:44.800
the feature is there,

28
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:45.540
ready to use,

29
00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.480
with nothing extra to install.

30
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.700
You might be thinking:

31
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.780
but I already have skills and connectors.

32
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.860
You do, and they’re still useful.

33
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.220
They just act from the outside.

34
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.400
A skill teaches Claude how to do

35
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.680
a task.

36
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.860
A connector links Claude to another app.

37
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.020
A mod is different:

38
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.420
it runs inside Claude Code itself.

39
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.700
That means it can change what you

40
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:07.280
see.

41
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.380
You can add a button,

42
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.680
open a side panel,

43
00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.660
create a bar above the text input,

44
00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:12.660
show an alert,

45
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.060
add a new slash command,

46
00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.800
or even make a little game to

47
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.040
pass the time while Claude works.

48
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.920
How can a mod change the interface?

49
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.540
Every time Claude does something,

50
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.000
like running a command,

51
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.740
calling a tool,

52
00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.200
or receiving your request,

53
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.580
it sends out a signal,

54
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:27.480
an event.

55
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.800
A mod is a small piece of

56
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.780
code that listens for those events.

57
00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:31.920
When one comes in,

58
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.260
it has three options.

59
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.620
It can simply observe and let things

60
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:36.220
continue.

61
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.560
It can change the event before anything

62
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:39.540
else sees it.

63
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.760
Or it can respond on its own,

64
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.900
so the normal behavior never happens.

65
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.300
For example, a mod can block a

66
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.660
dangerous command before it runs,

67
00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.800
or rewrite your request before it reaches

68
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.340
the model.

69
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.560
Let’s look at some examples,

70
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.340
inspired by Tristan’s video,

71
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.460
which introduced me to the topic.

72
00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.500
The first is a cleaner view.

73
00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.980
Many people don’t want to see every

74
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.420
tool call and line of code go

75
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:00.820
by.

76
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.540
They just want to know what’s being

77
00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.020
done, how much is left,

78
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.040
and what comes next.

79
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.480
The mod intercepts the technical lines,

80
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:09.600
hides them from view,

81
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.400
and shows a simple task list.

82
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.920
Each completed step gets a checkmark.

83
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:14.820
At the end,

84
00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.380
you get a short summary of what

85
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:16.960
was done.

86
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.140
The work is the same;

87
00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.840
what you see is different.

88
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.680
The second example is a control panel.

89
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.560
Today, to

90
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.640
switch models, you type slash model and

91
00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:28.940
choose from the list.

92
00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.640
To change how much Claude thinks,

93
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.900
you type slash effort.

94
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.700
It works, but it gets tiring.

95
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:36.620
With a mod,

96
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.480
model and effort become buttons on their

97
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.520
own bar, and the status line below

98
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.100
updates instantly.

99
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:43.880
It’s a small thing,

100
00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.860
but it’s exactly the kind of everyday

101
00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.160
friction a mod can fix in minutes.

102
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.840
The third example is an agent dock.

103
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.100
On a big task,

104
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.580
Claude splits the work among helper agents,

105
00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:57.080
copies of itself that each handle one

106
00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.080
part.

107
00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.800
The problem is that you usually can’t

108
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.520
see what each one is doing or

109
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:02.820
choose how many work at the same

110
00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:03.260
time.

111
00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.240
With the mod,

112
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.500
you choose the team size,

113
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.400
and each agent gets a card with

114
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.320
its task and a progress bar.

115
00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.260
At the end,

116
00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.760
Claude brings everything together into one result.

117
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.740
One important thing to keep in mind:

118
00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.860
a big team uses up your limit

119
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.720
much faster.

120
00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.400
A good mod warns you before you

121
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.400
go too far.

122
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.260
Two more examples.

123
00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.740
A mod can connect Claude Code to

124
00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.920
an image and video generation service using

125
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.340
a connector you already have.

126
00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:32.060
Here’s the part I liked most:

127
00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.700
before generating anything,

128
00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.380
it shows how many credits it will

129
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.080
use and waits for you to say

130
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:37.560
yes.

131
00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.040
That’s the kind of rule I want

132
00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.840
for anything that costs money.

133
00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.480
And you can make things just for

134
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.360
fun, like a little racing game in

135
00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.840
the terminal that occasionally asks you a

136
00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:48.340
question about Claude itself.

137
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.420
Better than staring at the screen while

138
00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:51.280
you wait.

139
00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.140
How do you create your own?

140
00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.060
First, update Claude Code.

141
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.140
Mods require a recent version;

142
00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:59.700
the one on my machine is

143
00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:01.530
2.1.289.

144
00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.450
Then just describe the mod you want.

145
00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:06.760
Claude Code comes with a skill that

146
00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:08.500
teaches it how to build mods.

147
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.220
It writes

148
00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.040
the files and asks

149
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.260
if you want to enable automatic reloading

150
00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.340
in this session.

151
00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:17.900
Say yes.

152
00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:18.980
When it’s done,

153
00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.000
the mod appears.

154
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.240
Don’t like something?

155
00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.780
Tell it the way you’d tell a

156
00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.880
person: what bothered you and what to

157
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:25.400
change.

158
00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.400
It adjusts the mod and reloads it.

159
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.320
Three things to keep in mind.

160
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.460
First: a mod Claude creates for you

161
00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:34.960
starts out as temporary,

162
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.780
tied to that session.

163
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:37.580
Like it?

164
00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.500
Ask Claude to save it as a

165
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.660
plugin and install it,

166
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.380
so it’s there every time you open

167
00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.720
Claude Code.

168
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.300
Second: a mod is a type of

169
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:46.960
plugin.

170
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.000
If someone shares one with you,

171
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.620
install it with the slash plugin command,

172
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.280
like any other plugin.

173
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.760
And the usual rule applies:

174
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.980
only install mods from trusted sources,

175
00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.900
because a mod runs inside your Claude

176
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.100
Code.

177
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.020
Third: mods have no extra cost,

178
00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.730
but you need a paid plan with

179
00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:06.500
Claude Code, and a mod that runs

180
00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.360
in the background also uses up your

181
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:08.640
limit.

182
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:09.800
To sum up:

183
00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.660
a mod is a feature you describe,

184
00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.700
and Claude Code builds it right inside

185
00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:14.440
itself.

186
00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.680
My suggestion is to start small:

187
00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.140
think of the thing that bothers you

188
00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.540
most in Claude Code today,

189
00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.260
a command you type all the time

190
00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.140
or information you wish you could always

191
00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.780
see, and ask for a mod to

192
00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:25.660
do that.

193
00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:28.240
Courses, guides, and kits about Claude Code

194
00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.340
and agents are on INEMA dot club,

195
00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.240
open and free.

196
00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:33.880
See you in the next video.
